    {
      "id": "ports-and-handles-as-authored-interface-geometry",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Projection Geometry",
      "position": {
        "x": 820,
        "y": 160
      },
      "width": 650,
      "height": 540,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ports-and-handles-as-authored-interface"
        },
        "markdown": "# Visible authored interface geometry\n\nLet the Title position be the structural center `C`. Let the Declaration position be `D`.\n\n`rootDirection = normalize(D - C)`\n\nThe Declaration-to-Title relationship establishes root orientation and the expected behavior of the instantiated root affordance. Its distance is authoring geometry; it does not have to define the rendered node's width or height.\n\nIn edit mode, project the instantiated node's outline at zoom 1 behind the graph, replacing the ordinary Twilite logo. Let that outline be `B`. For a Port at position `Q`:\n\n`direction = normalize(Q - C)`\n\n`boundaryPoint = intersectRay(C, direction, B)`\n\nOnly the Port's direction from Title determines its projected endpoint position. If that Port declares initiating behavior, the endpoint is rendered with a draggable handle affordance.\n\n## Layout domains\n\n- **Structural anchors:** Declaration and Title. Their relationship defines root orientation and root navigation focuses Title.\n- **Boundary participants:** Ports. Their directions are functional and protected from ordinary auto-layout.\n- **Projected frame:** the actual zoom-1 node outline used for authoring feedback, independently sized by presentation.\n- **Interior participants:** portals, documentation, examples, implementation nodes, and other movable content. Auto-layout may arrange these within the projected outline.\n- **Whole interface:** may translate or scale as a unit without changing normalized endpoint directions.\n\nThe same ray-intersection contract works for rectangles, rounded cards, circles, SVG silhouettes, and three-dimensional surfaces."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ports-and-handles-as-authored-interface-interface-map",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Authored Interface Map",
      "position": {
        "x": 820,
        "y": -500
      },
      "width": 650,
      "height": 500,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": false,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "bidirectional",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 210
        }
      ],
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "ports-and-handles-as-authored-interface"
        },
        "markdown": "# Authored node interface\n\n```text\n                    actionable Port *\n                                .---------------.\n                           Port *                 * actionable Port\n                               /  Portal  Content  \\\n                              |      o       o      |\n                              |       [Title]       |\n                              |          O          |\n                               \\                    /\n                                *------------------'\n                         [Declaration: root Port]\n```\n\n- **Title** is the structural center and root focus target.\n- **Declaration** owns the durable root Port; its relationship to Title establishes root orientation and expected root behavior, not literal node size.\n- **Port** is the single stable endpoint primitive. Its label is presentation only.\n- Acceptance and initiating behaviors are declared independently on a Port.\n- An actionable Port projects a draggable **handle affordance**; Handle is not a second primitive.\n- In edit mode, the node's zoom-1 outline is projected behind the graph and Ports intersect that boundary by authored direction.\n- Portals consume surfaces but remain ordinary movable interior content.\n- A drop reports source Port plus a target Port or graph coordinates; authored behavior decides what happens.\n- Relationship meaning remains on the edge or edge class."
      }
    },
